# ── Chapter 4 fidelity pins: "The Shield" ────────────────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/04-the-shield.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards

:expect-pins 27

# DON — "Don injured Pax. Then Don exposed Pax."
? injure(Don, Pax).
# => TRUE

? show(Don, Pax).
# => TRUE

# "Pax has none." — the condition that fails
? authority(Pax).
# => FALSE

# "Don has no shield and Don is a prisoner."
? defend(Don).
# => FALSE

? prisoner(Don).
# => TRUE

# SLY — "Sly injured Pax too. Then Sly exposed the court."
? injure(Sly, Pax).
# => TRUE

? show(Sly, Court).
# => TRUE

? authority(Court).
# => TRUE

# THE CONJUNCT THE SLY SECTION TURNS ON, and it was tested only through its
# result until now. Sly and Kel are name-swapped duplicates in the cast except
# for two facts, and this absence is one of them. `defend(Sly)` below would
# break if a deceit finding were written, so the protection existed — but the
# claim the chapter actually makes is about the ABSENCE, and an absence tested
# only downstream is an absence nobody has named.
? deceive(Sly, Court).
# => FALSE

# "So Sly's shield holds, and Sly ... walks free."
? defend(Sly).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Sly).
# => FALSE

# KEL — "Then the review body examined the exposure and found it deceitful."
? show(Kel, Court).
# => TRUE

? deceive(Kel, Court).
# => TRUE

# "Kel's shield falls ... Kel is a prisoner ... Kel's own credibility is voided."
# KEL RESOLVES ONLY BECAUSE KEL EXPOSED ONCE. These three are per-subject and a
# per-subject pin cannot see a per-PAIR defect — the shield rule is satisfied by
# ANY qualifying pair, so a second exposure reopens it. That is what the Rex
# block below exists to hold, and why it is not enough to pin Kel.
? defend(Kel).
# => FALSE

? prisoner(Kel).
# => TRUE

? false(Kel).
# => TRUE

# REX — "Rex did what Kel did, and then did one more thing."
# The disclosed cost of scoping the shield to the exposure. If any of these six
# flip, chapter 4's "Rex" section and the second item in "What it costs" are
# both wrong, and so is the constitution's E1d commentary.
? show(Rex, Court).
# => TRUE

? deceive(Rex, Court).
# => TRUE

# "Rex's credibility was destroyed by the same counter-audit."
? false(Rex).
# => TRUE

# "Then Rex exposed the review body. Nobody has examined that one."
? show(Rex, Review).
# => TRUE

# ...and the body Rex exposed has standing, which is the other half of "a fresh
# claim against an authority". `authority(Court)` is pinned five ways across the
# suite; its twin was pinned nowhere in the repo until now, so the second
# exposure's premise rested on nothing.
? authority(Review).
# => TRUE

# THE PAIR THAT MATTERS. Voided and shielded at once — the finding against the
# first exposure has nothing to say about the second.
? defend(Rex).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Rex).
# => FALSE

# ── THE REACH-BACK, MINUS THE CENTURY. MUST BE LAST: ground facts persist ────
# "A defendant a century from now can reach back, expose someone nobody
# remembers, and open the window." Executed, minus the century — which the flat
# record could not represent anyway: nothing below says whether Zeno's exposure
# came before or after Boss's recall, and that inexpressibility is one of the
# four reasons the closing section gives for refusing the bound. Boss is
# deliberately an existing name — reaching back to him is the exhibit — and
# everything else is fresh. One write blocks a conviction.
person(Zeno).
injure(Zeno, Yara).
judge(Court, Zeno).

? prisoner(Zeno).
# => TRUE

show(Zeno, Boss).

? prisoner(Zeno).
# => FALSE

? defend(Zeno).
# => TRUE

# ...and the man he reached back to was recalled long since. The shield does
# not ask.
? broken(Boss).
# => TRUE
